2.2 Assumptions in the Black-Scholes model
There are several idealized assumptions in the Black–Scholes model:

trading is possible continuously in time;
arbitrary fractions of the stock can be bought or sold;
short selling is allowed;
arbitrary amounts of money can be invested or borrowed at the same risk-free rate \(r\);
there are no transaction costs or taxes;
the stock pays no dividends;
the parameters \(\mu\), \(\sigma\), and \(r\) are constant.

These assumptions are clearly idealizations. Their purpose is to isolate the central mechanism behind the Black–Scholes model: continuous dynamic hedging.

3.1 The Black-Scholes formula







Theorem (The Black–Scholes formula).
Define \[
d_1(t,s)
=
\frac{
\ln(s/K)
+
\left(r+\frac12\sigma^2\right)(T-t)
}{
\sigma\sqrt{T-t}
},
\quad \text{ and }
d_2(t,s)
=
d_1(t,s)-\sigma\sqrt{T-t}.
\] Let \(N\) denote the cumulative distribution function of a standard normal random variable.
If there exist are no arbitrage opportunities in the Black-Scholes market, a european put and call option can be perfectly replicated by a hedging portfolio. The corresponding price and number of shares in the replicating portfolio are \[
\boxed{
P_t
=
Ke^{-r(T-t)}N\!\left(-d_2(t,S_t)\right)
-
S_tN\!\left(-d_1(t,S_t)\right).
}
\quad \text{ and }\quad
\boxed{
\Delta_t
=
N\!\left(d_1(t,S_t)\right)-1.
}
\] for a put option with strike \(K\) and maturity time \(T\) at time \(t&lt;T\). For the analogous call option, they are \[
\boxed{
C_t
=
S_tN\!\left(d_1(t,S_t)\right)
-
Ke^{-r(T-t)}N\!\left(d_2(t,S_t)\right),
}
\quad \text{ and }\quad
\boxed{
\Delta_t^{\mathrm{call}}
=
N\!\left(d_1(t,S_t)\right).
}
\]

Use of LLMs
You are welcome to use an LLM, such as ChatGPT, Claude, Le Chat, or a similar tool, to support your learning.
Used carefully, these tools can help explain mathematical notation, provide alternative explanations or generate additional examples. Most importantly, it can help to effortlessly translate and test statistical ideas into Python (or R, if you prefer).
A useful rule is:
You may use an LLM to help you learn, but you should be able to explain every statistical argument and every line of code that you use.
In a more general context, good uses of an LLM include asking it to:

explain a statistical concept in simpler language;
provide intuition for a definition or result;
give another example similar to one from class;
explain a step in a derivation after you have attempted it yourself;
help interpret an error message in Python or R;
suggest checks for a simulation study or statistical workflow;
generate additional practice questions;
compare two statistical approaches;
act as a coding assistant when you understand and verify the resulting code.

Poor uses include asking an LLM to:

solve exercises before you have attempted them yourself;
produce derivations that you cannot explain;
write code that you cannot read or justify;
choose a statistical method without checking its assumptions;
generate output that you submit without verifying it;
replace statistical reasoning with a long piece of automatically generated code.

After this chapter, you should be able to:

describe a probability space, a random variable, and its distribution;
work with probability mass functions, densities, and cumulative distribution functions;
recognize several important discrete and continuous distributions;
calculate and interpret expectations, variances, covariances, and selected moments;
distinguish population quantities from empirical quantities computed from a random sample;
distinguish convergence in probability from convergence in distribution;
explain the law of large numbers (LLN) and central limit theorem (CLT), including their different conclusions;
use Python simulations to investigate the finite-sample behavior behind these results.







TipHow to use this chapter



This is a refresher, not a first course in probability. We recall the central definitions and results, work through a few examples, and use simulations to connect mathematical statements to observable behavior. Try to predict the outcome of each experiment before running its code. The statistical interpretation is more important than the particular Python commands.


